1
00:00:07,120 --> 00:00:13,960
We came next to the Aeolian island; and there lived
Aeolus, Hippotas' son, dear to the deathless gods,

2
00:00:13,960 --> 00:00:19,820
on a floating island: all around it a wall of bronze,
unbreakable, and the cliff runs up sheer.

3
00:00:19,820 --> 00:00:24,780
Twelve children of his live in his halls,
six daughters and six sons in their prime,

4
00:00:24,780 --> 00:00:28,260
and he has given his daughters
to his sons to be their wives.

5
00:00:28,260 --> 00:00:30,900
They are always feasting beside their dear father

6
00:00:30,900 --> 00:00:35,180
and their honored mother, and good
things past counting are set before them;

7
00:00:35,180 --> 00:00:39,760
all day the house is savory with roasting,
and the courtyard rings with the sound;

8
00:00:39,760 --> 00:00:44,960
and at night they sleep beside their honored
wives among blankets, on corded bedsteads.

9
00:00:44,960 --> 00:00:48,400
To their city, then, we came, and their beautiful house.

10
00:00:48,400 --> 00:00:52,860
A full month he made much of me,
and questioned me on everything —

11
00:00:52,860 --> 00:00:59,000
Ilium, the Argives' ships, the Achaeans' homecoming —
and I told him all of it through, in its due order.

12
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:01,740
And when I in my turn asked for the road, and begged him

13
00:01:01,740 --> 00:01:05,640
to send me on, he refused me nothing:
he prepared my sending.

14
00:01:05,640 --> 00:01:09,220
He gave me a bag — the flayed hide of a nine-year ox —

15
00:01:09,220 --> 00:01:11,960
and in it he bound the courses of the blustering winds:

16
00:01:11,960 --> 00:01:17,540
for the son of Cronos made him steward of the winds,
to still them or to rouse them, whichever he will.

17
00:01:17,540 --> 00:01:20,240
In my hollow ship he lashed it fast with a shining cord

18
00:01:20,240 --> 00:01:24,080
of silver, so that not the
smallest breath could slip past;

19
00:01:24,080 --> 00:01:30,040
and for me he sent out the West Wind's breath to blow,
to carry the ships and the men. But it was not to be

20
00:01:30,040 --> 00:01:33,340
accomplished: we were destroyed by our own folly.

21
00:01:33,340 --> 00:01:38,900
Nine days and nights alike we sailed on, and on the
tenth our fathers' fields were rising into view —

22
00:01:38,900 --> 00:01:41,980
so near, we could see men tending their fires.

23
00:01:41,980 --> 00:01:45,360
Then sweet sleep came over me, worn out as I was:

24
00:01:45,360 --> 00:01:48,620
all that time I had worked the
ship's sheet myself, yielding it

25
00:01:48,620 --> 00:01:51,780
to no other companion, to bring us home the faster.

26
00:01:51,780 --> 00:01:57,440
And the companions began to talk to one another,
and said I was bringing home gold and silver for myself,

27
00:01:57,440 --> 00:02:00,460
gifts of great-hearted Aeolus, Hippotas' son.

28
00:02:00,460 --> 00:02:03,160
And thus would one of them say,
with a glance at his neighbor:

29
00:02:03,160 --> 00:02:09,120
'Look at him — how loved he is, how honored,
among all men, whatever city or land he comes to!

30
00:02:09,120 --> 00:02:11,880
He carries out of Troy a mass of beautiful treasure,

31
00:02:11,880 --> 00:02:17,760
his plunder — while we, who have accomplished
the same long road, come home with empty hands.

32
00:02:17,760 --> 00:02:20,760
And now Aeolus, obliging him in friendship, has given him

33
00:02:20,760 --> 00:02:24,120
this besides. Quick, then — let us see what is in it:

34
00:02:24,120 --> 00:02:27,260
how much gold, how much silver the bag holds.'

35
00:02:27,260 --> 00:02:30,260
So they talked, and the companions' evil counsel won.

36
00:02:30,260 --> 00:02:33,080
They opened the bag — and all the winds burst out,

37
00:02:33,080 --> 00:02:35,900
and the storm snatched them up
at once and swept them seaward,

38
00:02:35,900 --> 00:02:41,480
weeping, away from their fathers' land. And I,
waking, debated in my own blameless heart

39
00:02:41,480 --> 00:02:47,020
whether to drop from the deck and drown in the sea
or endure in silence and stay among the living.

40
00:02:47,020 --> 00:02:49,880
I endured, and I stayed. Covering my head

41
00:02:49,880 --> 00:02:54,700
I lay where I was; and the ships were
carried by the evil blast of the storm

42
00:02:54,700 --> 00:02:58,180
back to the Aeolian island; and my companions groaned.

43
00:02:58,180 --> 00:03:01,700
There we set foot on the mainland and drew water,

44
00:03:01,700 --> 00:03:05,480
and the companions quickly took
their meal beside the fast ships.

45
00:03:05,480 --> 00:03:10,780
And when we had tasted food and drink,
then I took with me a herald and one companion

46
00:03:10,780 --> 00:03:16,800
and went to the famous house of Aeolus.
I found him feasting beside his wife and his children.

47
00:03:16,800 --> 00:03:20,120
We came into the house and sat down by the doorposts

48
00:03:20,120 --> 00:03:24,140
on the threshold; and they were
amazed at heart, and asked:

49
00:03:24,140 --> 00:03:28,500
'How are you here, Odysseus? What evil power attacked you?

50
00:03:28,500 --> 00:03:31,160
Surely we sent you off with all care, to reach

51
00:03:31,160 --> 00:03:35,080
your fatherland and your house,
and whatever place is dear to you.'

52
00:03:35,080 --> 00:03:39,200
So they spoke; and I, grieving at heart, addressed them:

53
00:03:39,200 --> 00:03:45,880
'My evil companions ruined me — they, and a pitiless
sleep. But heal it, friends: the power is yours.'

54
00:03:45,880 --> 00:03:51,620
So I spoke, plying them with soft words;
and they fell silent. And the father answered:

55
00:03:51,620 --> 00:03:55,440
'Out of this island — fast — you vilest thing alive!

56
00:03:55,440 --> 00:04:01,760
It is against all right for me to harbor or to
send on his way a man the blessed gods detest.

57
00:04:01,760 --> 00:04:05,220
Out! — since you come here hated by the deathless ones.'

58
00:04:05,220 --> 00:04:09,620
So speaking he sent me from his house, groaning heavily.

59
00:04:09,620 --> 00:04:12,640
From there we sailed on further, grieved at heart.

60
00:04:12,640 --> 00:04:19,320
And the men's spirit was worn down by the grinding rowing —
our own fault: no sending wind showed any more.

61
00:04:19,320 --> 00:04:25,380
Six days and nights alike we sailed on,
and on the seventh we came to Lamus' steep citadel —

62
00:04:25,380 --> 00:04:28,880
Telepylus of the Laestrygonians,
where herdsman hails herdsman:

63
00:04:28,880 --> 00:04:32,520
the one driving in calls, and the one driving out answers.

64
00:04:32,520 --> 00:04:39,020
There a sleepless man could have earned a double wage,
one herding cattle, one pasturing silvery sheep:

65
00:04:39,020 --> 00:04:42,220
so close together are the paths of night and day.

66
00:04:42,220 --> 00:04:48,160
There, when we came into the famous harbor —
a sheer cliff runs unbroken all around it, on both sides,

67
00:04:48,160 --> 00:04:53,160
and jutting headlands, facing one another,
thrust out at the mouth, and the entrance is narrow —

68
00:04:53,160 --> 00:04:55,660
there all the others steered their curved ships in,

69
00:04:55,660 --> 00:04:58,420
and moored them inside the hollow harbor, close together:

70
00:04:58,420 --> 00:05:03,460
for no wave ever swelled in there, great or
small, but a white calm lay all around it.

71
00:05:03,460 --> 00:05:08,960
I alone held my black ship outside, there at
the edge, and made the cables fast to the rock;

72
00:05:08,960 --> 00:05:12,020
and I climbed a broken lookout-height and stood there.

73
00:05:12,020 --> 00:05:17,420
No works of oxen or of men showed anywhere;
we saw only smoke springing up from the land.

74
00:05:17,420 --> 00:05:22,240
Then I sent companions ahead to learn what
men they were who ate bread upon this earth:

75
00:05:22,240 --> 00:05:25,540
two men I chose, and sent a third with them as herald.

76
00:05:25,540 --> 00:05:28,480
They went ashore and took a
level road, by which the wagons

77
00:05:28,480 --> 00:05:34,400
hauled timber down to the city from the high mountains,
and before the city they met a girl drawing water,

78
00:05:34,400 --> 00:05:37,120
the sturdy daughter of Laestrygonian Antiphates.

79
00:05:37,120 --> 00:05:42,100
She had come down to the fair-flowing spring Artacia,
from which they carried water to the town;

80
00:05:42,100 --> 00:05:47,620
and they came up beside her and spoke to her,
and asked who was king of these people, and whom he ruled;

81
00:05:47,620 --> 00:05:51,160
and she pointed them at once to
her father's high-roofed house.

82
00:05:51,160 --> 00:05:53,960
But when they entered the famous
house, they found his wife

83
00:05:53,960 --> 00:05:57,220
huge as a mountain peak —
and they were sick with horror at her.

84
00:05:57,220 --> 00:06:00,540
She called famous Antiphates
straightway from the gathering-place —

85
00:06:00,540 --> 00:06:03,820
her husband — and he plotted their wretched destruction:

86
00:06:03,820 --> 00:06:07,740
he snatched up one of my companions
on the spot and made his meal of him,

87
00:06:07,740 --> 00:06:11,000
and the other two sprang away
and came flying to the ships.

88
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:13,960
Then he raised the war-cry
through the city; and hearing it

89
00:06:13,960 --> 00:06:17,000
the mighty Laestrygonians came thronging from every side,

90
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:20,580
thousands of them — not like men, but like the Giants.

91
00:06:20,580 --> 00:06:26,360
From the cliffs they pelted us with man-heavy stones,
and at once an evil din rose up along the ships —

92
00:06:26,360 --> 00:06:29,940
the scream of dying men, the crunch of shattering hulls.

93
00:06:29,940 --> 00:06:33,320
Spearing them like fish,
they carried home their loathsome meal.

94
00:06:33,320 --> 00:06:38,440
While they were slaughtering those inside the deep
harbor, I drew the sharp sword from beside my thigh

95
00:06:38,440 --> 00:06:43,760
and cut the cables of my dark-blue-prowed ship,
and quickly I roused my companions and ordered them

96
00:06:43,760 --> 00:06:46,320
to fall to their oars and get us clear of the evil;

97
00:06:46,320 --> 00:06:50,000
Gladly my ship fled out to open
water, away from the beetling

98
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:53,540
cliffs; but the others were destroyed there, all together.

99
00:06:53,540 --> 00:07:00,500
From there we sailed on further, grieved at heart,
glad to be out of death, having lost our dear companions.

100
00:07:00,500 --> 00:07:03,520
And we came to the island of Aeaea; and there lived

101
00:07:03,520 --> 00:07:10,200
Circe of the lovely braids, dread goddess with a
mortal's voice, own sister of Aeetes the deadly-minded:

102
00:07:10,200 --> 00:07:15,900
both born of Helios, who carries light to mortals,
and of Perse their mother, whom Ocean fathered.

103
00:07:15,900 --> 00:07:18,460
There we brought the ship silently to the shore

104
00:07:18,460 --> 00:07:21,880
and into a harbor fit for ships;
and some god was guiding us.

105
00:07:21,880 --> 00:07:25,320
We stepped ashore there, and for two days and two nights

106
00:07:25,320 --> 00:07:29,160
we lay, eating our hearts with
weariness and pain together.

107
00:07:29,160 --> 00:07:34,840
But when Dawn of the lovely braids brought the third
day round, then I took my spear and my sharp sword

108
00:07:34,840 --> 00:07:40,760
and went up quickly from the ship to a viewing-place,
in hope of seeing men's works, or catching a voice.

109
00:07:40,760 --> 00:07:43,600
I climbed a broken lookout-height and stood there,

110
00:07:43,600 --> 00:07:47,720
and smoke showed to my eyes,
rising from the wide-wayed earth

111
00:07:47,720 --> 00:07:50,620
in Circe's halls, up through
the dense brush and the timber.

112
00:07:50,620 --> 00:07:53,040
And I debated then in my mind and in my heart

113
00:07:53,040 --> 00:07:57,060
whether to go and look, since I
had seen the fire-bright smoke.

114
00:07:57,060 --> 00:08:00,140
And as I weighed it, this seemed to me the better way:

115
00:08:00,140 --> 00:08:02,960
to go first to the fast ship and the shore of the sea,

116
00:08:02,960 --> 00:08:06,260
give the companions their meal,
and send them out to learn.

117
00:08:06,260 --> 00:08:12,200
But when, on my way down, I was near the curved ship,
then some god took pity on me, alone as I was,

118
00:08:12,200 --> 00:08:15,560
and sent a great high-antlered stag into my very path.

119
00:08:15,560 --> 00:08:21,040
He was coming down to the river from his forest
pasture to drink, for the sun's force was heavy on him;

120
00:08:21,040 --> 00:08:24,400
and as he stepped out I struck him on the spine, mid-back:

121
00:08:24,400 --> 00:08:30,240
the bronze spear went clean through, and down he dropped
in the dust with a cry, and the life flew from him.

122
00:08:30,240 --> 00:08:35,180
I set my foot on him and drew the bronze spear out
of the wound, and laid it down there on the ground

123
00:08:35,180 --> 00:08:40,540
and left it; then I pulled brushwood and withies
and plaited a rope, a fathom's length, well-twisted

124
00:08:40,540 --> 00:08:44,000
all along, and bound together the feet of the huge beast,

125
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:47,960
and went down to the black ship
carrying him slung across my neck,

126
00:08:47,960 --> 00:08:54,080
leaning on my spear: there was no carrying him on the
shoulder with one hand — he was a very great beast.

127
00:08:54,080 --> 00:09:00,800
I flung him down before the ship, and woke my companions
with gentle words, standing by each man in turn:

128
00:09:00,800 --> 00:09:05,900
'Friends — for all our grief, we will not go down
to the house of Hades before our destined day.

129
00:09:05,900 --> 00:09:09,000
Come then, while there is food and drink in the fast ship,

130
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:12,280
let us think of eating, and not be worn away with hunger.'

131
00:09:12,280 --> 00:09:15,200
So I spoke, and they quickly heeded my words:

132
00:09:15,200 --> 00:09:18,160
they uncovered their heads there
by the shore of the barren sea

133
00:09:18,160 --> 00:09:21,120
and marveled at the stag: he was a very great beast.

134
00:09:21,120 --> 00:09:26,480
And when they had had their joy of gazing,
they washed their hands and made the glorious feast ready.

135
00:09:26,480 --> 00:09:32,560
So then all day long till the setting of the sun we
sat feasting on meat past counting and sweet wine;

136
00:09:32,560 --> 00:09:38,680
and when the sun went down and the dusk came on,
then we lay down to sleep on the surf-line of the sea.

137
00:09:38,680 --> 00:09:42,280
When Dawn appeared, the early-born, with fingers of rose,

138
00:09:42,280 --> 00:09:45,380
then I called an assembly, and spoke among them all:

139
00:09:45,380 --> 00:09:48,560
'Hear my words, companions, for all your suffering.

140
00:09:48,560 --> 00:09:52,160
Friends — we do not know where
the dark is, or where the dawn,

141
00:09:52,160 --> 00:09:55,420
or where the sun that carries light
to mortals goes under the earth,

142
00:09:55,420 --> 00:10:02,380
or where he rises. Let us think, then — quickly —
whether any device is left us. I do not believe there is.

143
00:10:02,380 --> 00:10:07,560
I climbed a broken lookout-height and saw an island,
wreathed round with the boundless sea;

144
00:10:07,560 --> 00:10:10,940
the island itself lies low,
and in the middle of it my eyes

145
00:10:10,940 --> 00:10:14,040
caught smoke, up through the dense brush and the timber.'

146
00:10:14,040 --> 00:10:19,980
So I spoke, and their own hearts broke within them,
remembering the deeds of Laestrygonian Antiphates

147
00:10:19,980 --> 00:10:23,080
and the violence of the great-hearted man-eating Cyclops.

148
00:10:23,080 --> 00:10:27,780
They wept aloud, letting the big tears fall —
but nothing came of all their mourning.

149
00:10:27,780 --> 00:10:34,080
Then I counted off all my strong-greaved companions
into two bands, and gave each band a leader:

150
00:10:34,080 --> 00:10:37,700
I led the one, and godlike Eurylochus the other.

151
00:10:37,700 --> 00:10:43,360
We shook the lots quickly in a bronze helmet,
and out jumped the lot of great-hearted Eurylochus.

152
00:10:43,360 --> 00:10:49,960
So he set out, and with him two-and-twenty companions,
weeping — and they left us behind in tears as well.

153
00:10:49,960 --> 00:10:52,960
In a forest glen they found the house of Circe, built

154
00:10:52,960 --> 00:10:59,420
of polished stones, in a place with a clear view all around;
and around it were mountain wolves and lions —

155
00:10:59,420 --> 00:11:03,200
creatures she herself had bewitched,
giving them evil drugs.

156
00:11:03,200 --> 00:11:05,720
And these did not spring at the men, no:

157
00:11:05,720 --> 00:11:09,200
they rose and fawned around them, waving their long tails.

158
00:11:09,200 --> 00:11:12,320
As when dogs fawn around their
master coming from a feast —

159
00:11:12,320 --> 00:11:17,840
for he always carries home some sweet morsel for them —
so the strong-clawed wolves and the lions fawned

160
00:11:17,840 --> 00:11:22,640
around them; and they were terrified at
the sight of the dread huge creatures.

161
00:11:22,640 --> 00:11:25,740
They stood in the gateway of
the goddess of the lovely hair,

162
00:11:25,740 --> 00:11:29,160
and inside they heard Circe singing in a beautiful voice,

163
00:11:29,160 --> 00:11:32,480
moving to and fro at her great
imperishable loom — the weaving

164
00:11:32,480 --> 00:11:36,920
goddesses do: fine-spun work, graceful and shining.

165
00:11:36,920 --> 00:11:40,400
And Polites began to speak among them — a leader of men,

166
00:11:40,400 --> 00:11:44,620
who of all my companions was closest and dearest to me:

167
00:11:44,620 --> 00:11:46,780
'Friends — someone inside there, moving at a great loom,

168
00:11:46,780 --> 00:11:53,940
is singing, beautifully — the whole floor hums with it —
goddess or woman. Quick, let us call out to her.'

169
00:11:53,940 --> 00:11:56,780
So he spoke, and they lifted their voices and called.

170
00:11:56,780 --> 00:11:59,660
And she came out at once, and opened the shining doors,

171
00:11:59,660 --> 00:12:02,980
and invited them in; and all together, in their innocence,

172
00:12:02,980 --> 00:12:07,900
they followed her. Only Eurylochus
stayed behind, suspecting a snare.

173
00:12:07,900 --> 00:12:10,800
She led them in and seated them on benches and chairs,

174
00:12:10,800 --> 00:12:14,140
and stirred them a mixture:
cheese and barley meal and pale honey

175
00:12:14,140 --> 00:12:17,120
in Pramnian wine. And into the dish she blended

176
00:12:17,120 --> 00:12:21,200
baleful drugs, to make them
wholly forget their fathers' land.

177
00:12:21,200 --> 00:12:24,580
And when she had served it and
they had drunk it down, at once

178
00:12:24,580 --> 00:12:28,140
she struck them with her wand
and penned them in the pigsties.

179
00:12:28,140 --> 00:12:31,280
And they had the heads of pigs — the voice, the bristles,

180
00:12:31,280 --> 00:12:35,320
the body — but their minds
stayed steady, exactly as before.

181
00:12:35,320 --> 00:12:40,880
So they were penned there weeping; and Circe threw
them acorns of oak and ilex, and cornel berries,

182
00:12:40,880 --> 00:12:43,740
the things pigs eat, the wallowers on the ground.

183
00:12:43,740 --> 00:12:47,460
But Eurylochus came running back to the fast black ship

184
00:12:47,460 --> 00:12:50,980
to bring the news of the companions and their bitter fate.

185
00:12:50,980 --> 00:12:55,180
And he could not get one word out, for all his straining —

186
00:12:55,180 --> 00:13:02,020
struck to the heart with his great anguish; his eyes
brimmed full of tears, and his spirit was all lament.

187
00:13:02,020 --> 00:13:05,300
But when we questioned him, all of us, in our amazement,

188
00:13:05,300 --> 00:13:08,560
at last he told the destruction of the other companions:

189
00:13:08,560 --> 00:13:12,800
'We went up through the brush,
as you commanded, glorious Odysseus,

190
00:13:12,800 --> 00:13:15,920
and in a forest glen we found a beautiful house, built

191
00:13:15,920 --> 00:13:20,100
of polished stones, in a place
with a clear view all around.

192
00:13:20,100 --> 00:13:24,460
And there someone at a great loom
was singing, clear and high —

193
00:13:24,460 --> 00:13:28,120
goddess or woman. And they lifted their voices and called.

194
00:13:28,120 --> 00:13:30,900
And she came out at once, and opened the shining doors,

195
00:13:30,900 --> 00:13:33,960
and invited them in; and all together, in their innocence,

196
00:13:33,960 --> 00:13:38,700
they followed her. But I stayed
behind, suspecting a snare.

197
00:13:38,700 --> 00:13:41,720
And they vanished — all of them together. Not one of them

198
00:13:41,720 --> 00:13:45,280
appeared again, though I sat a long time watching.'

199
00:13:45,280 --> 00:13:48,300
So he spoke; and I slung my silver-studded sword

200
00:13:48,300 --> 00:13:52,340
about my shoulders — the great
bronze one — and my bow with it,

201
00:13:52,340 --> 00:13:55,080
and ordered him to lead me back by the same way.

202
00:13:55,080 --> 00:14:01,500
But he seized me with both hands, clasping my knees,
and wailing he spoke winged words to me:

203
00:14:01,500 --> 00:14:06,460
'Do not take me back there — Zeus-nurtured —
against my will. Leave me here.

204
00:14:06,460 --> 00:14:09,540
For I know it: you will not come
back yourself, and you will not bring

205
00:14:09,540 --> 00:14:15,660
one of your companions. No — with these men here,
quickly, let us run: we may still escape the evil day.'

206
00:14:15,660 --> 00:14:18,700
So he spoke, and I answered him and said:

207
00:14:18,700 --> 00:14:22,700
'Eurylochus — stay here, then, where you are,
eating and drinking beside the hollow black ship.

208
00:14:22,700 --> 00:14:25,980
But I am going. Hard necessity is on me.'

209
00:14:25,980 --> 00:14:28,580
So speaking I went up from the ship and the sea.

210
00:14:28,580 --> 00:14:34,540
But just as, threading the sacred glens, I was about
to reach the great house of Circe of the many drugs,

211
00:14:34,540 --> 00:14:39,720
there Hermes of the golden rod met me as I came
toward the house — in the likeness of a young man

212
00:14:39,720 --> 00:14:43,480
with his first down on his lip,
in the loveliest season of youth.

213
00:14:43,480 --> 00:14:47,060
He clamped a hand on mine, and spoke a word, and named me:

214
00:14:47,060 --> 00:14:50,600
'Where now, poor doomed man, alone along the heights,

215
00:14:50,600 --> 00:14:53,940
a stranger to the country?
Your companions there in Circe's house

216
00:14:53,940 --> 00:14:57,340
are penned like pigs, packed in their hidden sties.

217
00:14:57,340 --> 00:15:00,940
Is it to free them you are going?
You will not come back yourself,

218
00:15:00,940 --> 00:15:04,520
I tell you: you will only stay there with the others.

219
00:15:04,520 --> 00:15:07,740
But come — I will release you
from the evil, I will save you.

220
00:15:07,740 --> 00:15:13,500
Here. Take this good drug with you into Circe's house —
it will keep the evil day away from your head.

221
00:15:13,500 --> 00:15:16,300
And I will tell you all the deadly designs of Circe.

222
00:15:16,300 --> 00:15:19,680
She will mix you a potion, and cast drugs into the dish;

223
00:15:19,680 --> 00:15:23,060
but even so she will not have
power to bewitch you: the good drug

224
00:15:23,060 --> 00:15:26,340
I give you will not let her.
And I will tell you everything.

225
00:15:26,340 --> 00:15:32,180
The moment Circe strikes at you with her long wand,
that instant draw the sharp sword from beside your thigh

226
00:15:32,180 --> 00:15:34,820
and go for Circe as though you meant to kill her.

227
00:15:34,820 --> 00:15:38,540
She will be seized with terror, and invite you to her bed.

228
00:15:38,540 --> 00:15:44,460
Do not, then, refuse the bed of the goddess —
if you want her to free your companions and host you well —

229
00:15:44,460 --> 00:15:47,360
but order her to swear the great oath of the blessed ones

230
00:15:47,360 --> 00:15:50,300
that she is not planning some new evil against you:

231
00:15:50,300 --> 00:15:54,780
lest she strip you naked and make
you a coward, an unmanned thing.'

232
00:15:54,780 --> 00:16:00,700
So speaking, the slayer of Argus handed me the drug,
pulling it out of the ground, and showed me its nature:

233
00:16:00,700 --> 00:16:03,740
at the root it was black, but its flower like milk.

234
00:16:03,740 --> 00:16:09,660
The gods call it. And it is hard for mortal men to dig —
but the gods have power to do all things.

235
00:16:09,660 --> 00:16:15,220
Then Hermes went away over the wooded island toward
high Olympus; and I went on to the house of Circe,

236
00:16:15,220 --> 00:16:17,960
and my heart ran dark with many things as I went.

237
00:16:17,960 --> 00:16:21,180
I stopped at the doors of the goddess of the lovely hair,

238
00:16:21,180 --> 00:16:25,040
and standing there I shouted;
and the goddess heard my voice.

239
00:16:25,040 --> 00:16:28,460
And she came out at once, and opened the shining doors,

240
00:16:28,460 --> 00:16:33,280
and invited me in; and I followed her, grieved at heart.

241
00:16:33,280 --> 00:16:37,260
She led me in and seated me on a silver-studded chair,

242
00:16:37,260 --> 00:16:40,680
beautiful, cunningly worked, with a footstool for my feet,

243
00:16:40,680 --> 00:16:45,140
and made me a potion in a golden cup, for me to drink,

244
00:16:45,140 --> 00:16:48,660
and slipped a drug in it, with evil purpose in her heart.

245
00:16:48,660 --> 00:16:52,980
And when she served it and I drank
it off — and it did not bewitch me —

246
00:16:52,980 --> 00:16:57,140
she struck me with her wand,
and spoke a word, and named me:

247
00:16:57,140 --> 00:17:01,240
'Off to the sty now. Lie down
with the rest of your companions.'

248
00:17:01,240 --> 00:17:05,340
So she spoke; and I drew the
sharp sword from beside my thigh

249
00:17:05,340 --> 00:17:08,600
and went for Circe as though I meant to kill her.

250
00:17:08,600 --> 00:17:16,240
And she shrieked, and ran under the blade, and seized
my knees, and wailing she spoke winged words to me:

251
00:17:16,240 --> 00:17:20,820
'What man are you, and from where?
Where are your city and your parents?

252
00:17:20,820 --> 00:17:24,860
Wonder holds me: you drank these
drugs and were not bewitched —

253
00:17:24,860 --> 00:17:27,940
and no other man, no, not one, has withstood these drugs

254
00:17:27,940 --> 00:17:32,240
once he drank them, once they
passed the fence of his teeth.

255
00:17:32,240 --> 00:17:35,460
The mind in your breast is proof against enchantment.

256
00:17:35,460 --> 00:17:39,140
Surely you are Odysseus — the man of many turnings — whom

257
00:17:39,140 --> 00:17:42,500
the golden-rodded slayer of
Argus always told me would come,

258
00:17:42,500 --> 00:17:45,740
on his way home from Troy in his fast black ship.

259
00:17:45,740 --> 00:17:51,920
Come now — put your sword in its sheath, and let the
two of us mount my bed together, so that mingled there

260
00:17:51,920 --> 00:17:55,680
in the bed of love, we may come to trust one another.'

261
00:17:55,680 --> 00:17:59,300
So she spoke, and I answered her and said:

262
00:17:59,300 --> 00:18:02,200
'Circe — how can you bid me be gentle with you?

263
00:18:02,200 --> 00:18:08,340
You who turned my companions into pigs in your own halls,
and keep me here, and — scheming still — invite me

264
00:18:08,340 --> 00:18:15,400
to your chamber, to climb into your bed, so that
stripped naked I may be made a coward, an unmanned thing.

265
00:18:15,400 --> 00:18:20,900
I will not consent to climb into your bed unless you
bring yourself, goddess, to swear me the great oath

266
00:18:20,900 --> 00:18:24,620
that you are not planning some new evil against me.'

267
00:18:24,620 --> 00:18:28,520
So I spoke, and she swore it once, as I demanded.

268
00:18:28,520 --> 00:18:35,520
And when she had sworn, and made an end of the oath,
then I mounted the surpassingly beautiful bed of Circe.

269
00:18:35,520 --> 00:18:41,600
Meanwhile four handmaids were busy through the halls —
the serving-women who keep her house in order,

270
00:18:41,600 --> 00:18:46,180
daughters of the springs and the groves and
the sacred rivers that flow out to the sea.

271
00:18:46,180 --> 00:18:51,860
One of them threw beautiful covers over the chairs,
purple above, with linen spread beneath;

272
00:18:51,860 --> 00:18:56,860
another drew silver tables up before the
chairs and set out golden baskets on them;

273
00:18:56,860 --> 00:19:02,540
the third mixed honey-hearted wine, sweet wine,
in a silver bowl, and set out golden cups;

274
00:19:02,540 --> 00:19:08,840
and the fourth brought water and kindled a great
fire under a huge cauldron, and the water warmed.

275
00:19:08,840 --> 00:19:15,420
And when the water boiled in the bright bronze,
she set me in the tub and bathed me from the great cauldron,

276
00:19:15,420 --> 00:19:19,180
tempering it to my liking,
pouring it over my head and shoulders,

277
00:19:19,180 --> 00:19:22,840
until she had taken the heart-wasting
weariness from my limbs.

278
00:19:22,840 --> 00:19:26,220
And when she had bathed me and rubbed me richly with oil,

279
00:19:26,220 --> 00:19:32,900
and thrown a beautiful cloak around me, and a tunic,
she led me in and seated me on a silver-studded chair,

280
00:19:32,900 --> 00:19:35,860
beautiful, cunningly worked, with a footstool for my feet;

281
00:19:35,860 --> 00:19:38,920
and a handmaid brought water for my hands in a pitcher,

282
00:19:38,920 --> 00:19:42,040
a beautiful golden one, and poured it over a silver basin

283
00:19:42,040 --> 00:19:46,160
for me to wash, and drew up a polished table beside me.

284
00:19:46,160 --> 00:19:49,300
The grave housekeeper brought bread and set it before me,

285
00:19:49,300 --> 00:19:53,060
laying out delicacies in plenty,
giving gladly of her store,

286
00:19:53,060 --> 00:19:56,160
and pressed me to eat. But my heart was not in it:

287
00:19:56,160 --> 00:20:00,000
I sat with my thoughts elsewhere,
and my spirit boded evil.

288
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:05,640
And when Circe saw me sitting there, not reaching
my hands to the bread, gripped in a strong grief,

289
00:20:05,640 --> 00:20:08,800
she came near, and spoke winged words to me:

290
00:20:08,800 --> 00:20:12,620
'Why do you sit like this,
Odysseus, like a man struck dumb,

291
00:20:12,620 --> 00:20:15,940
eating your heart, and touching neither food nor drink?

292
00:20:15,940 --> 00:20:21,900
Do you suspect some other snare? You have no cause
to fear: I have already sworn you the strong oath.'

293
00:20:21,900 --> 00:20:25,180
So she spoke, and I answered her and said:

294
00:20:25,180 --> 00:20:29,060
'Circe — what man with any decency in
him could bear to taste of food and drink

295
00:20:29,060 --> 00:20:33,060
before his companions were set free,
and he saw them with his eyes?

296
00:20:33,060 --> 00:20:39,700
If you press me in good faith to eat and drink,
then free them — let my eyes see my trusted companions.'

297
00:20:39,700 --> 00:20:45,140
So I spoke, and Circe went out through the hall,
wand in hand, and opened the doors of the sty,

298
00:20:45,140 --> 00:20:48,200
and drove them out —
in the shape of nine-year fattened hogs.

299
00:20:48,200 --> 00:20:52,820
They stood there facing her; and she went
among them anointing each with another drug;

300
00:20:52,820 --> 00:20:55,600
and the bristles the baleful drug had grown on them —

301
00:20:55,600 --> 00:20:58,880
the drug the lady Circe gave them —
dropped from their limbs,

302
00:20:58,880 --> 00:21:04,760
and they were men again — younger than they were before,
far more beautiful, and taller to the eye.

303
00:21:04,760 --> 00:21:10,780
They knew me; and each man clung to my hands;
and a passionate weeping came over us all, and the house

304
00:21:10,780 --> 00:21:14,540
rang terribly around us; and the
goddess herself was moved to pity.

305
00:21:14,540 --> 00:21:19,680
She came close beside me and spoke,
shining among goddesses:

306
00:21:19,680 --> 00:21:27,740
'Zeus-born son of Laertes, Odysseus of the many devices —
go now to your fast ship and the shore of the sea.

307
00:21:27,740 --> 00:21:34,760
First of all, haul the ship up onto dry land,
and stow your goods and all the tackle in the caves;

308
00:21:34,760 --> 00:21:38,800
then come back yourself,
and bring your trusted companions.'

309
00:21:38,800 --> 00:21:42,220
So she spoke, and my proud heart consented.

310
00:21:42,220 --> 00:21:45,820
I went my way to the fast ship and the shore of the sea,

311
00:21:45,820 --> 00:21:52,600
and there by the fast ship I found my trusted companions
in pitiful lamentation, letting the big tears fall.

312
00:21:52,600 --> 00:21:58,580
As when farmyard calves around the herded cows that
come back to the dung-yard, filled with grazing,

313
00:21:58,580 --> 00:22:04,560
all skip and frisk to meet them — the pens no
longer hold them, but lowing without pause they run

314
00:22:04,560 --> 00:22:08,260
around their mothers: so those men,
when their eyes took me in,

315
00:22:08,260 --> 00:22:10,980
poured around me weeping. And to their hearts it felt

316
00:22:10,980 --> 00:22:17,300
as if they had come to their fatherland, to their very
town in rugged Ithaca, where they were bred and born.

317
00:22:17,300 --> 00:22:20,420
And wailing, they spoke winged words to me:

318
00:22:20,420 --> 00:22:26,920
'At your return, Zeus-nurtured, we rejoice as if
we had come home to Ithaca, our fathers' land.

319
00:22:26,920 --> 00:22:30,460
But come — tell the destruction of the other companions.'

320
00:22:30,460 --> 00:22:33,960
So they spoke, and I answered with soft words:

321
00:22:33,960 --> 00:22:39,700
'First of all, let us haul the ship up onto dry land,
and stow our goods and all the tackle in the caves;

322
00:22:39,700 --> 00:22:43,040
then bestir yourselves, all of you, and come with me,

323
00:22:43,040 --> 00:22:46,400
so you may see our companions
in the sacred halls of Circe,

324
00:22:46,400 --> 00:22:49,420
eating and drinking: what they have never fails.'

325
00:22:49,420 --> 00:22:52,060
So I spoke, and they quickly heeded my words.

326
00:22:52,060 --> 00:22:58,400
Only Eurylochus tried to hold back all my companions,
and lifting his voice he spoke winged words to them:

327
00:22:58,400 --> 00:23:02,740
'Poor fools — where are we going?
Why do you hanker after these evils —

328
00:23:02,740 --> 00:23:08,020
to go down into Circe's hall, who will turn
every one of us into pigs or wolves or lions

329
00:23:08,020 --> 00:23:11,240
to guard her great house for her — and guard it we must —

330
00:23:11,240 --> 00:23:13,800
the way the Cyclops did his work, when our companions

331
00:23:13,800 --> 00:23:17,440
came to his inner yard,
and the reckless Odysseus with them:

332
00:23:17,440 --> 00:23:20,240
it was that man's blind folly killed them too.'

333
00:23:20,240 --> 00:23:23,320
So he spoke; and I turned it over in my mind —

334
00:23:23,320 --> 00:23:26,720
whether to draw the long-edged
sword from beside my thick thigh,

335
00:23:26,720 --> 00:23:29,380
strike off his head and bring it down in the dust,

336
00:23:29,380 --> 00:23:33,360
kinsman by marriage though he was,
and a close one. But my companions

337
00:23:33,360 --> 00:23:37,040
held me back with honeyed words, this one and that one:

338
00:23:37,040 --> 00:23:42,680
'Zeus-born — this man, if you command it, we will
leave here by the ship, to stay and guard the ship;

339
00:23:42,680 --> 00:23:45,640
lead the rest of us to the sacred house of Circe.'

340
00:23:45,640 --> 00:23:51,420
So speaking they went up from the ship and the sea;
and Eurylochus was not left behind by the hollow ship —

341
00:23:51,420 --> 00:23:52,400
he followed.

342
00:23:52,400 --> 00:23:56,740
Meanwhile, in her house,
Circe had bathed the other companions

343
00:23:56,740 --> 00:24:03,080
with kind care, and rubbed them richly with oil,
and thrown about them fleecy cloaks and tunics,

344
00:24:03,080 --> 00:24:06,020
and we found them all feasting well in the halls.

345
00:24:06,020 --> 00:24:09,540
And when the men saw one another,
face to face, and knew each other,

346
00:24:09,540 --> 00:24:13,060
they wept and lamented, and the house rang round them.

347
00:24:13,060 --> 00:24:17,480
And she came close beside me and
spoke, shining among goddesses:

348
00:24:17,480 --> 00:24:23,960
'No more of this swelling lament now. I know it myself —
all you have suffered on the fish-filled sea,

349
00:24:23,960 --> 00:24:26,940
all the harm lawless men have done you on dry land.

350
00:24:26,940 --> 00:24:32,320
Come now: eat your food, and drink your wine,
until you gather back the spirit in your breasts

351
00:24:32,320 --> 00:24:39,100
that was yours when you first left your fathers' land
of rugged Ithaca. Now you are withered, spiritless,

352
00:24:39,100 --> 00:24:41,760
forever brooding on the misery
of your wandering — your hearts

353
00:24:41,760 --> 00:24:45,320
never in the feast, for truly you have suffered much.'

354
00:24:45,320 --> 00:24:47,920
So she spoke, and our proud hearts consented.

355
00:24:47,920 --> 00:24:54,700
So there, day after day, for the full round of a year,
we sat feasting on meat past counting and sweet wine.

356
00:24:54,700 --> 00:24:57,840
But when the year was out,
and the seasons turned in their wheel,

357
00:24:57,840 --> 00:25:03,360
as the months waned and the long days came round,
then my trusted companions called me out, and said:

358
00:25:03,360 --> 00:25:06,820
'You are possessed! It is time
to remember your fatherland —

359
00:25:06,820 --> 00:25:12,300
if it is truly decreed that you be saved, and come
to your high-roofed house and your own fatherland.'

360
00:25:12,300 --> 00:25:14,960
So they spoke, and my proud heart consented.

361
00:25:14,960 --> 00:25:20,760
So then all day long till the setting of the sun we
sat feasting on meat past counting and sweet wine;

362
00:25:20,760 --> 00:25:26,120
and when the sun went down and the dusk came on,
the men lay down to sleep through the shadowy halls,

363
00:25:26,120 --> 00:25:29,180
but I climbed into the surpassingly beautiful bed of Circe

364
00:25:29,180 --> 00:25:34,020
and clasped her knees in supplication;
and the goddess heard my voice.

365
00:25:34,020 --> 00:25:37,740
And lifting my voice I spoke winged words to her:

366
00:25:37,740 --> 00:25:43,220
'Circe — make good now the promise that you made me,
to send me home. My spirit strains for it already,

367
00:25:43,220 --> 00:25:48,580
and my companions' too: they wear my heart away,
mourning around me whenever you are elsewhere.'

368
00:25:48,580 --> 00:25:52,820
So I spoke, and she answered at once,
shining among goddesses:

369
00:25:52,820 --> 00:25:56,080
'Zeus-born son of Laertes, Odysseus of the many devices:

370
00:25:56,080 --> 00:25:59,240
stay no longer in my house against your will.

371
00:25:59,240 --> 00:26:02,740
But there is another journey you must
accomplish first: you must go down

372
00:26:02,740 --> 00:26:08,120
to the house of Hades and dread Persephone
to consult the ghost of Theban Tiresias,

373
00:26:08,120 --> 00:26:10,920
the blind seer, whose mind stands fast:

374
00:26:10,920 --> 00:26:14,020
to him, even dead, Persephone has granted understanding —

375
00:26:14,020 --> 00:26:18,300
to him alone the power of thought.
The rest flit past as shadows.

376
00:26:18,300 --> 00:26:23,520
So she spoke, and my heart broke within me,
and I wept, sitting there on the bed; my heart

377
00:26:23,520 --> 00:26:26,560
had no more will to live and see the light of the sun.

378
00:26:26,560 --> 00:26:32,060
But when I had had my fill of weeping and writhing,
then I answered her with words, and said:

379
00:26:32,060 --> 00:26:34,360
'Circe — and who will be pilot on that voyage?

380
00:26:34,360 --> 00:26:37,000
No man ever yet reached Hades in a black ship.'

381
00:26:37,000 --> 00:26:41,220
So I spoke, and she answered at once,
shining among goddesses:

382
00:26:41,220 --> 00:26:45,060
'Zeus-born son of Laertes, Odysseus of the many devices:

383
00:26:45,060 --> 00:26:47,960
let no lack of a pilot at the ship concern you.

384
00:26:47,960 --> 00:26:54,020
Set up the mast, spread the white sails open,
and sit: the breath of the North Wind will carry her.

385
00:26:54,020 --> 00:26:56,720
But when in your ship you have
crossed the stream of Ocean,

386
00:26:56,720 --> 00:27:00,320
there is a scrub shore there,
and the groves of Persephone —

387
00:27:00,320 --> 00:27:04,100
tall black poplars, and willows
that shed their fruit unripened,

388
00:27:04,100 --> 00:27:10,240
Beach the ship there, on Ocean's deep-eddying edge,
and go on yourself into the dank house of Hades.

389
00:27:10,240 --> 00:27:15,960
There into Acheron pour Pyriphlegethon and Cocytus,
which is a branch broken off the water of Styx;

390
00:27:15,960 --> 00:27:19,500
and there is a rock,
at the meeting-roar of the two rivers.

391
00:27:19,500 --> 00:27:27,040
Draw close to it there, hero, as I command you,
and dig a pit about a cubit's measure this way and that,

392
00:27:27,040 --> 00:27:30,180
and around it pour a drink-offering to all the dead —

393
00:27:30,180 --> 00:27:33,640
first with milk and honey mingled, then with sweet wine,

394
00:27:33,640 --> 00:27:38,040
the third time with water;
and sprinkle white barley over it.

395
00:27:38,040 --> 00:27:41,500
And entreat, over and over,
the strengthless heads of the dead:

396
00:27:41,500 --> 00:27:44,340
vow that, come to Ithaca, you will offer in your halls

397
00:27:44,340 --> 00:27:48,680
a barren heifer, your best,
and heap the pyre with good things;

398
00:27:48,680 --> 00:27:52,120
and to Tiresias, apart, alone, you will sacrifice a ram

399
00:27:52,120 --> 00:27:56,060
all black, the one that stands out from all your flocks.

400
00:27:56,060 --> 00:28:02,160
Then, when with prayers you have entreated the glorious
tribes of the dead, sacrifice a ram and a black ewe,

401
00:28:02,160 --> 00:28:05,660
bending their heads toward Erebus —
but turn yourself away,

402
00:28:05,660 --> 00:28:11,820
facing the river's streams. And there will come,
in crowds, the souls of the dead who are gone.

403
00:28:11,820 --> 00:28:14,100
Then rouse your companions, and command them

404
00:28:14,100 --> 00:28:19,860
to flay the sheep that lie there slaughtered by the
pitiless bronze, and burn them, and pray to the gods —

405
00:28:19,860 --> 00:28:22,380
to mighty Hades and dread Persephone.

406
00:28:22,380 --> 00:28:25,260
And you: draw the sharp sword from beside your thigh

407
00:28:25,260 --> 00:28:31,240
and sit, and do not let the strengthless heads of the
dead come near the blood before you question Tiresias.

408
00:28:31,240 --> 00:28:34,420
And quickly then the seer will come, leader of peoples,

409
00:28:34,420 --> 00:28:37,280
and he will tell you the road,
and the measures of your journey,

410
00:28:37,280 --> 00:28:41,480
and your homecoming — how you
shall go over the fish-filled sea.'

411
00:28:41,480 --> 00:28:45,660
So she spoke; and at once Dawn
of the golden throne came on.

412
00:28:45,660 --> 00:28:49,140
She dressed me then in my clothes, a cloak and a tunic,

413
00:28:49,140 --> 00:28:52,980
and she, the nymph, put on a great silver-shining mantle,

414
00:28:52,980 --> 00:28:56,600
fine-woven and graceful, and cast about her waist a belt,

415
00:28:56,600 --> 00:29:00,080
beautiful, golden, and set a veil on her head above.

416
00:29:00,080 --> 00:29:05,700
And I went through the halls and roused my companions
with gentle words, standing by each man in turn:

417
00:29:05,700 --> 00:29:08,420
'No more lying now, drowsing in sweet sleep.

418
00:29:08,420 --> 00:29:11,440
Let us go: the lady Circe has shown me the way.'

419
00:29:11,440 --> 00:29:14,800
So I spoke, and their proud hearts consented.

420
00:29:14,800 --> 00:29:18,300
But not even from there could I
bring my companions off unharmed.

421
00:29:18,300 --> 00:29:24,380
There was one, Elpenor, the youngest of all —
no great fighter in war, no steady mind on him —

422
00:29:24,380 --> 00:29:28,160
who had lain down apart from his
companions in the sacred house of Circe,

423
00:29:28,160 --> 00:29:30,640
heavy with wine, wanting the cool air.

424
00:29:30,640 --> 00:29:33,000
Hearing the noise and stir of his companions moving,

425
00:29:33,000 --> 00:29:39,260
he leapt up all at once — and forgot, in his fuddled wits,
to come back down again by the long ladder;

426
00:29:39,260 --> 00:29:45,240
he fell headlong from the roof, and his neck snapped
from the spine; and his soul went down to Hades.

427
00:29:45,240 --> 00:29:47,760
And as my men were setting out I spoke among them:

428
00:29:47,760 --> 00:29:50,160
'You think, no doubt, that you are going home, to your own

429
00:29:50,160 --> 00:29:53,960
dear fatherland. But Circe has
marked out another road for us:

430
00:29:53,960 --> 00:29:59,000
to the house of Hades and dread Persephone,
to consult the ghost of Theban Tiresias.'

431
00:29:59,000 --> 00:30:02,240
So I spoke, and their own hearts broke within them:

432
00:30:02,240 --> 00:30:05,940
they sat down right where they were,
and wailed, and tore their hair —

433
00:30:05,940 --> 00:30:07,540
but nothing came of all their mourning.

434
00:30:07,540 --> 00:30:14,200
And while we walked to the fast ship and the shore
of the sea, grieving, letting the big tears fall,

435
00:30:14,200 --> 00:30:19,700
Circe meanwhile had gone down, and beside the
black ship had tethered a ram and a black ewe —

436
00:30:19,700 --> 00:30:25,872
slipping past us with ease. Who could see a god
against the god's will, moving here or there?
